{
 "_comment": "One point per polity in domains.json, used only to place a mark on the braille map. The point is the polity's seat of government, chosen because it is a mechanical fact rather than a judgement: no centroid, no population weighting, no 'most important city'. Where the seat is contested or split, the rule is applied literally and the choice is recorded in _notes rather than hidden. A mark on the map says 'a source published from this polity', never anything about the place itself. Positions on the map are derived from data/map/coastline.json's own projection fields, so this file carries latitude and longitude and nothing pre-computed.",
 "_notes": {
  "Bolivia": "La Paz: seat of government. Sucre is the constitutional capital.",
  "Israel": "Jerusalem: seat of government. The mark records where a publisher publishes from and takes no position on the city's status.",
  "Palestine": "Ramallah: administrative seat. Same reading as above.",
  "Netherlands": "Amsterdam: constitutional capital. The government sits in The Hague.",
  "South Africa": "Pretoria: executive capital, of three.",
  "Sri Lanka": "Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte: legislative and judicial seat, adjacent to Colombo.",
  "Hong Kong": "Not a state. It is in the table because it is a distinct publishing polity (#20).",
  "Taiwan": "Taipei. Listed as a publishing polity, which is the only claim the axis makes."
 },
